During the past four weeks several of the leading religious denominations of Tennessee have held their annual conventions or conferences. The reports which have been submitted tell of an abundant harvest during the twelve months past. Every department of the church work has prospered during that time. People have given more literally to the support of the gospel; for its propagation in the foreign fields and for the maintenance of religious institutions than during any previous year in the history of the church. Although by no means as well compensated as they should be, the preachers are better paid than ever before; as a rule they are better housed. The religious edifices have been repaired, many new ones have been erected and in short there has been a notable advance in nearly all fields of religious endeavor.

And better than all this there has been an increase in the church membership. The Methodists at Franklin reported net gain in the bounds of the Tennessee Conference of 5000 or about five per cent of the present membership. While the exact statistics of the other denominations are not available from the tenor of the preliminary reports it is certain that there has been a substantial gain in the membership.

These reports are most encouraging. They are an emphatic and a convincing answer to the pessimist who continually complains that the world is degenerating. He magnifies every evil and looks at all the blessings through a darkly colored glass. Somehow things are not like they used to be. And they are not. The people are spending thousands for the spread of the gospel, for the dissemination of the blessings of the church, for the uplift of humanity and for the advancement of civilization where a few years ago they were spending hundreds. Of course a man's religion is not to be measured by his tendency toward the contribution basket, but it is nevertheless a fact that the purse is seldom touched unless the heart has first been moved. The Christian life is a life of service and there are few who give for the amelioration of the conditions of life;

